  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing CSS Imports**\n\nOkay, I\'ve dived into `index.css`. Specifically, I\'m examining how to enable class-based dark mode using Tailwind CSS v4. The key seems to be including a `@custom-variant dark` or `@variant dark` directive. My attention is now focused on the exact implementation details, particularly the proper scope of the variant \342\200\223 ensuring it correctly targets elements within the `dark` context.\n\n\n**Investigating Dark Mode Implementation**\n\nOkay, I\'ve confirmed that Tailwind v4\'s default `dark:...` compiles to `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`, which forced the whole app into dark mode. I found that a `@custom-variant` with `&:(where(.dark, .dark *))` resolves this. I am now checking for the `isDarkMode` state and its toggling of the `\'dark\'` class on the `<html>` element and `localStorage`. I\'m also examining the tabs to see why they\'re not switching correctly.\n\n\n**Examining Component Styling**\n\nI\'m now closely reviewing the styling within the tab navigation bar and the `TripSummaryHeader`. I need to ensure the dark mode styles are correctly applied to the tab navigation bar and the following `TripSummaryHeader`. I\'m especially focused on the shadow and background color transitions within these components and how they look in the `dark` state. I\'m also ensuring the contrast is sufficient to guarantee readability.\n\n\n**Clarifying Page Layout**\n\nOkay, I\'ve identified a key usability issue. The current tab implementation feels broken because the core content (schedule, map, etc.) is *below* the Hero Banner, Action Toolbar and tab buttons. Switching tabs doesn\'t appear to *do* anything since the visible part of the screen remains identical. My thought is to move the summary header and toolbar to the top of the content, right under the tab bar, to ensure that the content refreshes correctly.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 616244603
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
